        "id": "s1_habitat_pellicle",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Habitat and pellicle",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Pellicle",
        "lines": [
          "Euglenoids are mostly fresh water organisms",
          "found in stagnant water.",
          "In place of a cell wall, the body has a",
          "protein-rich layer called the pellicle.",
          "The pellicle makes the body flexible."
        ],
        "why": "The pellicle is the defining covering of this group: because it is protein and not a rigid wall, the cell can change its shape as it moves.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing that euglenoids have a cellulose cell wall. They have no cell wall at all.",
          "Leaving out flexible — that property is why the pellicle is worth naming."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "No wall — pellicle: a protein layer, so the body is flexible.",
        "margin_note": "Habitat plus the pellicle point; protein-rich is the exact term."
      },
      {
        "id": "s2_flagella_parts",
        "kind": "text",
        "label": "Flagella and cell parts",
        "marks": 1,
        "mark_note": "Flagella, parts",
        "lines": [
          "There are two flagella — one short, one long.",
          "The anterior part has a cytostome,",
          "a cytopharynx and a reservoir.",
          "An eye spot lies on the membrane",
          "of the reservoir."
        ],
        "why": "These are the structures the diagram must also show: the paired flagella for movement, the anterior gullet parts, and the light-sensitive eye spot beside the reservoir.",
        "common_mistakes": [
          "Writing one flagellum. The pair is one short plus one long.",
          "Placing the eye spot at the posterior end; it lies near the reservoir at the anterior end."
        ],
        "memory_tip": "Front of the cell in order: cytostome, cytopharynx, reservoir — eye spot on the reservoir.",
